Zebra Technologies Printers Remote/Unauthenticated Reflected XSS in Web Interface Alert Messaging System
(See related vulnerability:https://gist.github.com/lethalcyanide/0c1044ee9261f9ffc67a02f2eb80ba4c + https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2024-55094 as I believe there is likely complete overlap between printers vulnerable to that CVE and printers with the vulnerability I have discovered.)

Known Vulnerable Versions:
• ZTC ZT411-203dpi ZPL printer (firmware version: 92.21.27Z, link-os version:6.8.1). 
• ZTC ZT610-300dpi ZPL printer (firmware version: 80.20.10Z, link-os version: 5.0.0). 
• ZTC ZT410-203dpi ZPL printer (firmware version: 75.20.14z, link-os version: 5.0.0). 
• ZTC ZD411-203dpi ZPL printer (firmware version: 93.21.16z, link-os version: 6.7.0). 

(NB: There are likely others and I did not have access to other devices to test them. These versions are older and the vulnerabilities may be fixed in a newer version. It may be prudent to check rather than to assume this does not apply to your printer model/version.)

The Vulnerability:
The identified printers have several reflected XSS vulnerabilities in them (which means that you generally need to trick someone into clicking a malicious link with an XSS payload in it to trigger the vulnerability). In addition to those that lethalcyanide identified, which are applicable to the versions that I have identified above, there is an additional XSS vulnerability in the /unsadd page of the web interface (which is used for setting up alerts). The "pw" field strangely incorporates the web interface password into the HTML of the page without any sanitization, making it possible to execute arbitrary JavaScript in the browser of a victim that clicks on a malicious link. An example payload would be:

Example Implications:
An attacker may execute arbitary JavaScript in a victim's browser. An example of what this could be used for that is seemingly not applicable in this case is session cookie exfiltration. The identified printers appeared to use an IP-based whitelist for tracking successful authentication rather than session cookies (which itself may have security implication(s) I will not address here since I did not test for it/them), which means that cookie exfiltration is not a primary concern. Instead, I could see this being used to do any of the following:
--Replace the intended web page content with arbitrary content in service of a social engineering campaign or to induce the victim to download malware
--Silently redirect the victim to an attacker-controlled server in service of a social engineering campaign or to induce the victim to download malware
--Replace the intended page content or modify it so that intended data and functionality appear to be unavailable (which, since this is a reflected XSS vulnerability, can be rectified by browsing to a URL without the included payload
--In niche/extreme cases, browser exploitation may be possible

Disclosure Timeline:
This was disclosed to Zebra in January 2026. Multiple subsequent requests for permission to disclose in June 2026 went unanswered.
